 misidentified  i want to show you two examples of cell  lines to give you an idea of the  scientific as well as the financial  implications once misidentified cell  lines are used for studies  due to a contamination in the 1960s both  of these cell lines are  now known to be composed of hela cells  meaning scientists who thought  they were working on hab2 and in 407  were in fact likely experimenting on  healer cells it was calculated that over  700 million dollars has been spent  on published work involving just these  two contaminated cell lines  and the amount of money spent on  subsequent work based on those papers is  even much higher and these are just two  examples that show you how  that cell line authentication is a  crucial thing  um okay so before we start with our q a  session i hand over to christian again  yeah thank you jessica thank you eric  for this great presentation i've  recognized that we have a lot of people  joining us today live  so i guess there will be a lot of  question for our q a session but  before we will start with the last poll  question and the q a session  i promised you in the beginning that you  will get a quick insight into some  helpful solutions for your daily work  and i  will show you in a second um so here are  some uh  product solutions that you might not be  aware of  um from eppendorf the first one is our  co2 incubator family  cell expert that has been launched quite  um  about a year ago and that might be  interesting for you  so this is what it looks like and i  would just highlight some of the  the main benefits here so it's very easy  to clean  if you look inside the chamber there's  no fan  there's a deep drawn chamber inside and  one of the things that we've  taken care of is a very high temperature  homogeneity inside the incubator  so we measured at 27 spots inside the  incubator the temperature homogeneity so  it doesn't really matter where you  put your um your cell culture vessels  inside the incubator you  always the cells always experience the  same  cell culture conditions um one thing  that is also  important in modern cell culture labs  especially for  instruments that are running 24 7  are the running costs or costs that are  related to  um to to internal parts that have  been have to be exchanged  over time or especially gas consumption  which is a real  important topic and if you're interested  in this on  uh how you can save money with the co2  incubators you can see on the left side  of your screen  if you go to the resources section you  find a paper on cost of ownership for  cto incubators and also  a paper on or a little flyer on how the  co2 incubator cell expert can help you  to  optimize your cell-based essay  all right one thing that's also new um  is uh  are the 25 ml conical tubes that we  offer which you can see here  um it's especially useful for improving  uh contamination prevention in the cell  culture lab because you all know  that if you don't have steady hands on  uh on monday morning  it's really hard to pipe that into 15 ml  or 50 ml conicals without touching the  the rim or the the rim of the of the  vessels inside  so it helps you a lot if you have those  uh those tiny 25 ml tubes to to prevent  the contamination  if you want to learn more and if you  want to request a free sample  just go to eppendorf.com 25 ml  one last thing i've mentioned before is  our newsletter inside cell culture it's  called  if you want to get regular information  for cell culture  professionals you get tips and tricks  for your daily work videos downloads  posters and more you also get  access to advanced educational webinars  and also if you want to stay updated  upcoming webinars  just sign in go to eppendorf.com icc  and just type in your name and your  email address and you're ready  to go all right  so go one slide  further if you um so let's come to the  last port question here  um so we can improve our webinars for  you  so the question is how do you rate the  content of today's  webinar we highly appreciate your  feedback here  and we go directly to the live q a  section of this webinar so if you have  any  question that you would like to ask eric  or jessica  please do so now just click on the ask  question box located on the far left of  your screen  and we will answer as many of the  questions as we have time for  so let's get started just  choose a question let's have a look at  this  so one question that we have is  um  let's see i think this one will be good  for jessica we use a multi pad  uh not a multi-channel pipette for  seedling cells do you have any tips for  using that  to get the same amount of cells in wells  jessica this one what do you think uh  good question  um yeah so we um we checked different  let's say pipetting tools and how they  how they can be used for seeding cells  and with a multi-pad which is basically  a dispenser  there is no need to resuspend or to  swirl the cells up in between as long as  you're fast enough so you can be very  fast with pipetting or with  with a with a dispenser um  and seeding out cells and therefore you  don't need to mix the cells  like you like you would do with a  multi-channel pipette  and a reagent reservoir or even a single  channel pipette and the  the cell suspension in the tube so with  a dispenser of course it needs a little  bit of practice  uh with the handling but um with the  dispenser you can easily  fill a 96 well plate completely um and  have an equal number of  of cells in each well yeah thank you  very much  um let's move on there's um an  interesting one that's probably uh  directed towards eric uh regarding  the toxicity markers and the question is  regarding the ldh release how long can i  store the aliquots from the cell culture  supernatant  eric yeah thank you for that question  um here it is important that you  dilute your sample in a storage buffer  that is mentioned  in the the technical manual before you  freeze them  because this buffer helps to preserve  the activity of ldh  what we've tested so far is to store  samples up to one month at -20  and we did not note any decrease in the  activity of ldh  or any change in the ec50 values of  compounds that we treated the cells with  and i can imagine that probably also you  can store them for longer times but  the the one month is the time frame that  we've tested  oh thank you very much so we're there a  lot of questions coming in wow  um let's choose another one so this  sounds also interesting and the question  is  how long does it take for cells to  sediment in a reagent reservoir after  mixing the cell suspension one minute  less is that the time available for  seeding a plate before mixing in  and the question is how do you mix a  cell suspension in a reagent reservoir  type heading up and down shaking in an  eight formation  who wants to take this uh yes so  maybe first how do you mix the cell  suspension so  i would recommend to simply pipe it up  and down to mix the cells  because um shaking the reservoir always  uh has the risk that you have splashes  and you don't want to have that under  the bench  so when you resuspend so when you pipe  it up and down  what what a lot of people do  is they simply overdo it so pipetting up  and down  maybe three to four times is completely  sufficient um  uh to mix the cells and you don't have  to do it like 10 times because then you  have the shear forces again that might  stress the cells  and then the question how long does it  take for the cells to sediment that is a  little bit different to  uh difficult to answer because that will  depend on the on the volume and on the  on the cell number in the cell  suspension so um  you can say it maybe takes a few minutes  until you would see it with the naked  eye  if you have a clear reservoir but  um the the the cells already start to  to sink towards the bottom before so i  would  recommend for a 96 well plate resuspend  two to three times every second row that  you are pipetting and then you can be  can be when you are in a good range of  a good mixing um frequency if you are  not sure if that is sufficient  for your cell concentration then you can  um just try it out and and  and count the the number of cells that  you see that in the first row and  compare it  with the number of cells in the in the  last row so  um then you can be sure if the if the  cell number is  um homogeneous over the whole plate  yeah thank you jessica i hope this this  answers this long question here comes an  uh  shorter question that is  dedicated to eric so the question is  are the essays that you have been  showing us before also applicable to  3d culture eric  well primarily these essays have been  designed for 2d culture  the non-lytic assays that are shown such  as the  the ldh glow essay or that real-time  viability essay  they can be used with 3d cultures  we we've used them successfully for 3d  models like  spheroids if it's a lytic assay  such as the cell tita glow assay then of  course the limiting  factor for using them with 3d cultures  is  the the lysis condition because you have  to  achieve a thorough lysis of a spheroid  for example  so you have to make sure that the assay  is achieving a thorough lysis of your  sample with regard to the cell tidal  glow assay we have  a version that is optimized for 3d  called cells header glow 3d  assay here the lysis strength  and the protocol for that assays have  been  optimized but in general every lytic  assay  could be applicable to 3d cultures  you just have to make sure that the the  lysis is thorough  yeah thank you eric i think this uh this  answers the question  um let's go to the next one i have one  that's probably going to jessica's  direction so the question is  our incubator has a fan inside so how  can i protect the cells from vibrations  caused by the fan  well basically um you cannot when you  when you have a  fan inside the incubator um  it cannot be avoided that the fan um is  switched on it is usually  i think it's dependent on which exact  device you have but it's usually  switched on after a door opening so and  also in between when the door is closed  so you cannot avoid that  so depending on where the fan is located  inside the chamber i would just try to  put myself  as far away as possible from the  location of that fan to  to reduce the vibration  as much as possible but when the  incubator  has a fan there is no way to yeah to to  avoid that the cells will be in  infected and affected in some way by  this vibration  yeah thank you very much i think it's  also important to  to monitor yourselves and see if there's  any effect on the location inside the  incubator if you  if you see any differences there  so the next question um that we have is  do the substances you use in the  real-time assays have any toxic effect  on the cells i've heard that a lot  before also in  form and i think this one goes to eric  yeah i think so too um no they don't not  well at least for for the time frame  that we've tested circles 72 hours  we didn't see any toxic effect the way  we check for this is that we  pre-incubated cells for that given time  frame with the assay reagents and we  then use these cells in a  subsequent assay and  compare these ic50 values that we for  example determined within  toxic compound with non-pre-treated  cells  and we did not see any difference  so at least for that time frame it's not  toxic but i'm pretty sure you could  expand that also for  you could incubate yourself also for  longer time frames but  that's the time window we tested because  we think the 72 hours  is also a time window that you would  usually run an essay  for because if you think of how many  cells you would have after three days  it's probably not applicable to to run  an assay longer than 72 hours  yeah and i think if you do um you just  use the same control as you do to be  sure that there are  no toxic effects if you use um run an  essay  longer than 72 um 17 to all right  yeah so let's go to the next question we  have a lot of questions still coming in  um so  let's choose this one here how to detect  eukaryotic  contamination how to avoid it is a  question from laurent  wants to take this one jessica yes um  well it's it's difficult to to  to detect eukaryotic contamination just  by looking at the cells because  um as i mentioned unless the cell  morphology  of the two cross-contaminated cell lines  is not drastically different  um you you can can hardly  see that there's a a cell line  contaminating um  your cell so um whenever the cells  suddenly  look different than you're used to then  i would  um would pay attention but what you can  actually do  to really detect the eukaryotic  contamination  is an str analysis so scr stands for  short tandem repeats and this is the  gold standard method  to detect cross-contamination or to  authenticate  a cell line and the question how to  avoid  you carry contamination there's a few  things  to um yeah that way you can can pay  attention to so one would be  to have separate um media bottles for  each cell and  and only work with one cell line at a  time  in the biosafety cabinet  [Music]  disinfect the the workspace in between  switching the cell lines and never use  any reagents or any um  media bottles aliquots or whatever for  two different  um for two different cell lines so keep  it as separate as possible  yeah jessica thank you very much i think  we're a little bit over time but there  are so many questions and we  think you're still still out there we  carry on for  maybe another two minutes so another  question that we have  is um probably going to eric's direction  the question is can i only perform  real-time measurements if my reader  instruments  is equipped with an atmospheric control  eric  no it is also possible if you don't have  an ac  the the minimal requirement is really  that you have a heating function  in order to maintain that constant  temperature  if you do not have a co2 supply you what  you need to do is you have to switch  your cell culture medium from a  bicarbonate buffer to a co2  independent medium these media use a  different buffer usually that's  heaps because otherwise your your ph  of the medium will simply or increase  over time so it will become  basic or turn pink as i mentioned before  yeah so thank you eric i think  this line next question will be the last  question for today um  we are um we will answer all of your  questions  afterwards and email you but now let's  go to the last question that is coming  from  petra from the university of ghent and  the question is  how do you defrost cells from -80 by  applying heat shock for a few seconds  or with warm medium you want to take  this one  um yes thank you for that question so  first of all  you should never um store  cells for a long time or store  eukaryotic cells for a long time at  minus 80. so  um the the long-term storage temperature  for eukaryotic cells should always be  um at least minus 120 150  minus 80 should not be should not be  used for longer than a few days  so i would i would recommend to to  to saw the cells as quickly as possible  because  you usually use a cryoprotectant when  you freeze the cells  most commonly as dmso and this is  cytotoxic  when the when in non-frozen state so  you want to make sure that you get rid  of the dmso or deluded as soon as  possible  so you can  if you do a heat shock you of course  have to  have to check whether this heat shock  harms the cell  so just do a viability  check maybe 48 hours after thawing  and yeah so i would just dilute the  cells with pre-warmed medium  and then it depends a little bit on the  cell type you're working with  you can either centrifuge the cells  right after thawing  to get rid of the dmso immediately or  you can  dilute the uh the cells  and um um uh changed the medium the next  day so for some  some cell lines are more sensitive to  even low concentration of the mso  and some are more sensitive to the  centrifugation step so if you don't know  what is the method of choice for your  cell line  i would simply compare both methods  and then see how the cells behave  yeah thank you jessica thank you also  eric for answering these questions  this was um quite a fast right through  the  q and a session so we have received over  let's  have a look over 200 questions that will  all be answered so thank you again  jessica  and eric do you have any final comments  for our audience  um thank you all for joining our webinar  today and  um yeah we as as christian said we will  follow up  any questions via email and within the  next days so  yeah goodbye from my side and take care  yeah also i would like to thank you for  joining the webinar  um if you have any questions  popping up later on you can also contact  us  us via email yeah stay healthy and thank  you  so before we go i would like to thank  you all for joining us today and for  your interesting questions  um as we said questions where we did not  have the time  for today and those that were submitted  during the on-demand period will be  addressed by the speakers  via the contact information you provided  at the end of the registration  i would like to thank jessica and eric  again for their time today and for  their brilliant presentation for sharing  their experience  we would also like to thank chrissy  jewell and the lab roots team for  supporting us today  and i'd like to make sure that you know  that this webcast can be viewed on  demand  and lab roots will alert you via email  when it's available for replay and we  encourage you all to share this email  with your colleagues who may have missed  the  webinar today keep in mind to have a  look at the inside cell culture  newsletter to stay updated on all  upcoming cell culture webinar  so we say goodbye from hamburg have a  nice day and most importantly  stay happy stay healthy and hel